cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  relfiledelivery: what a file transfer costs over an intermittent link,
  measured rather than reimplemented
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-24
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  A measurement harness over declared link conditions, not a protocol
  implementation. It drives deferred-NAK, immediate-NAK and unacknowledged
  transfer against a seeded model of a declared link -- forward and return bit
  rates, one-way light time, residual per-unit loss, a two-state Gilbert burst
  outage process and a contact plan -- and reports delivery-time quantiles with
  the tail stated separately, retransmission overhead and per-contact completion
  probability. cfdp-py and spacepackets implement the CCSDS File Delivery
  Protocol and are named and recommended for the protocol itself; neither is
  installed in the environment these numbers were measured in and no figure here
  is taken from either. Validation is level 2. The closed-form expected delivery
  time under geometric loss is exact and is the reference the simulation is
  checked against: the simulated mean agrees with it to within 1.9000 sampling
  standard errors over twelve declared grid cells, worst relative discrepancy
  1.537594e-02, and the forward transmission count agrees with n/(1-p) to 1.2187
  standard errors over twenty. The completion invariant, delivered bytes equal
  source bytes verified by SHA-256, is checked inside the simulator on every
  completed run rather than reported as a statistic: zero mismatches over 1829
  delivered runs of 2400 across four files, five link regimes and three modes,
  with fifteen of fifteen deliberately injected single-bit corruptions caught by
  the same check. Retransmission accounting closes against an independent
  per-unit attempt count on 1680 of 1680 runs. Delivery time is reported as
  quantiles because the mean is not a location statistic here: on a continuous
  link with eight per cent loss, not one run of three thousand lands within five
  per cent of its own sample mean, since the distribution is quantised by
  retransmission rounds and the mean falls in a gap between two modes. A learned
  delivery-time predictor, seven gradient-boosted quantile regression models on
  log delivery time, is benchmarked against the closed form and against a
  measured-quantile lookup table on 1127 held-out scenarios from a disjoint seed
  range. The closed form wins where its assumptions hold, by a factor of 2.0160
  in median absolute relative error, 0.051928 against 0.104687, using no
  training data at all; the learned model wins in the correlated-outage regime
  by a factor of 1.2623, 0.292259 against 0.368928. Both results are the
  specification's own prediction and both are published as measured. No
  uncertainty output is calibrated at nominal 0.90: measured coverage is 0.8146
  for the learned interval, 0.5093 for the closed form and 0.8784 for the lookup
  table, the last of which is the only one inside the declared band and which
  pays for it with an interval 8.57 times wider than the learned model's. The
  analytic baseline was strengthened before publication -- an earlier extension
  divided by the product of two duty cycles and was wrong by a factor of 6.14 on
  the worked example -- and the learned model's margin fell from 1.9036 to
  1.2623 as a result. Six defects were found during the build and all are
  recorded where they were found, including a catastrophic cancellation in the
  expected-round-count series that 777 tests did not catch and a validation
  check comparing a computed bound against an independent derivation did. All
  training data is synthetic, generated by a seeded script whose equations are
  printed in its module docstring; no flight data is used anywhere. Research
  grade. Not flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational
  aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - satellite-communications
  - file-delivery
  - intermittent-link
  - retransmission
  - delivery-time-distribution
  - contact-window
  - monte-carlo
  - quantile-regression
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Gilbert
        given-names: "E. N."
    title: "Capacity of a burst-noise channel"
    journal: "Bell System Technical Journal"
    volume: 39
    issue: 5
    year: 1960
    notes: >-
      The two-state burst-loss process, implemented in continuous time as
      relfiledelivery.link.OutageModel. It reproduces a mean outage duration and
      a duty cycle by construction and nothing else; every outage result in this
      repository is a statement about that process.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Feller
        given-names: "W."
    title: >-
      An Introduction to Probability Theory and Its Applications, volume 1
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 1968
    notes: >-
      Chapter XIII for the geometric distribution of the number of attempts
      until success, which gives the exact expected transmission count n/(1-p)
      implemented as relfiledelivery.analytic.expected_total_transmissions and
      measured against simulation to 1.2187 sampling standard errors.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Johnson
        given-names: "N. L."
      - family-names: Kemp
        given-names: "A. W."
      - family-names: Kotz
        given-names: "S."
    title: "Univariate Discrete Distributions"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 2005
    notes: >-
      Chapter 5 for the negative binomial as the sum of independent geometric
      variables, which gives the exact transmission-count quantile implemented
      as relfiledelivery.analytic.transmissions_quantile and verified against
      200000 draws to within one transmission.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Friedman
        given-names: "J. H."
    title: "Greedy function approximation: a gradient boosting machine"
    journal: "Annals of Statistics"
    volume: 29
    issue: 5
    year: 2001
    notes: >-
      The gradient boosting behind the learned quantile predictor, fitted with
      the quantile loss at seven levels on the base-10 logarithm of the delivery
      time.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Strobl
        given-names: "C."
      - family-names: Boulesteix
        given-names: "A.-L."
      - family-names: Zeileis
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Hothorn
        given-names: "T."
    title: >-
      Bias in random forest variable importance measures: illustrations, sources
      and a solution
    journal: "BMC Bioinformatics"
    volume: 8
    year: 2007
    notes: >-
      Why the impurity-based importances reported for the learned model are a
      description of the fitted model and not evidence about the link.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Pedregosa
        given-names: "F."
      - family-names: Varoquaux
        given-names: "G."
      - family-names: Gramfort
        given-names: "A."
    title: "Scikit-learn: machine learning in Python"
    journal: "Journal of Machine Learning Research"
    volume: 12
    year: 2011
    notes: >-
      The GradientBoostingRegressor implementation behind the learned component.
      Bounded to >=1.9,<1.10 in pyproject.toml because the measured coverage and
      error figures are properties of its fitted trees.
  - type: standard
    title: "CCSDS File Delivery Protocol (CFDP)"
    institution:
      name: "Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems"
    number: "CCSDS 727.0-B-5"
    year: 2020
    notes: >-
      The source of the transfer-mode names only. Nothing in this repository
      implements CFDP, no PDU here is wire-conformant, and no conformance claim
      is made. cfdp-py and spacepackets implement the protocol.
  - type: standard
    title: "Secure Hash Standard (SHS)"
    institution:
      name: "National Institute of Standards and Technology"
    number: "FIPS 180-4"
    year: 2015
    notes: >-
      SHA-256, used purely as a collision-resistant content identity for the
      completion invariant and for no security property.
